00:06:53.856 --> 00:06:59.029
So why does AI matter for
accessibility in WordPress?

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.515
Well, this may seem kind of obvious,
but it's a good place to start our

00:07:03.515 --> 00:07:09.103
conversation because the reality
for many WordPress practitioners and

00:07:09.117 --> 00:07:14.014
agencies is that we work on small
teams or we're solopreneurs like I am.

00:07:14.522 --> 00:07:18.507
And in, in doing that, we
have to wear many hats.

00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:25.336
In addition to the testing, the
auditing, the design, the development,

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:30.733
consulting work, uh, and training we do
as accessibility professionals, we often

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:36.907
need to handle our own sales and marketing
efforts, and we need to, uh, deal with

00:07:36.932 --> 00:07:44.851
ancillary but also very important tasks
of HR, finance, and, uh, legal concerns.

00:07:45.769 --> 00:07:51.184
And as you've no doubt heard and
experienced, AI can replace lots

00:07:51.185 --> 00:07:56.118
of repetitive work and speed
up structured tasks, uh, which

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:01.013
allows smaller organizations and
solopreneurs like you and me to

00:08:01.026 --> 00:08:06.271
grow our impact and scale without
having to increase headcount so much.

00:08:07.245 --> 00:08:13.947
Now, one drumbeat I'm going to
hit over and over during this time

00:08:13.948 --> 00:08:21.433
together is that efficiency cannot
replace thoughtful accessibility work.

00:08:21.716 --> 00:08:26.584
Um, and so that needs to be said up
front, and it needs to come into every

00:08:27.204 --> 00:08:31.132
conversation and thought process we
have about artificial intelligence

00:08:33.759 --> 00:08:37.756
With that said, I wanna talk about
AI's relationship to accessibility

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.171
'cause it's a bit complicated.

00:08:40.164 --> 00:08:46.626
Uh, as we've heard and the hype
machines have, uh, told us for

00:08:46.642 --> 00:08:50.362
months and months now, AI is really
helping, and that's very true.

00:08:50.993 --> 00:08:57.516
AI can automate some repetitive
accessibility work, um, like testing

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:01.784
and faster audit reporting, uh,
just to name a couple of things.

00:09:02.001 --> 00:09:09.855
One of the most interesting pieces
of, um, of accessibility advantage, I

00:09:09.856 --> 00:09:14.726
will say, uh, that AI has en-enabled
is this thing that Anthropic is

00:09:14.727 --> 00:09:17.249
calling disability as infrastructure.

00:09:17.569 --> 00:09:22.074
Anthropic, uh, surveyed about eighty-one
thousand of its users a few months ago,

00:09:22.517 --> 00:09:28.084
and part of their findings were that,
um, people with disabilities are actually

00:09:28.087 --> 00:09:35.555
using AI to write specific tools and
solutions for problems they are facing

00:09:35.699 --> 00:09:40.478
to get around accessibility barriers,
if you will, and overcome obstacles

00:09:40.689 --> 00:09:47.524
or complete tasks that were otherwise
challenging or impossible for them.

00:09:47.921 --> 00:09:53.685
And this is because, you know, with a
little bit of know-how, uh, many, many

00:09:53.685 --> 00:10:00.069
people are now able to use AI-assisted
coding to, uh, create software solutions.

00:10:00.528 --> 00:10:04.660
And up till this point in
history, uh, it wasn't economical

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:10.020
for each person to develop a
software solution for themselves.

00:10:10.136 --> 00:10:13.544
Um, but now it-- now that
is becoming mainstream.

00:10:14.443 --> 00:10:18.156
We are able to build things to
solve our own problems, and that's

00:10:18.157 --> 00:10:26.053
a really, uh, neat and optimistic,
promising, uh, feature of AI.

00:10:27.271 --> 00:10:35.948
Conversely, AI is also hurting,
uh, accessibility because, um, AI

00:10:35.969 --> 00:10:41.598
auto-generates inaccessible code,
websites, and docs really by default, and

00:10:41.598 --> 00:10:43.269
we'll talk about why that is in a second.

00:10:43.884 --> 00:10:48.478
Um, it will often build UIs
without really thinking about

00:10:48.483 --> 00:10:50.125
s-the semantics underneath of them.

00:10:50.924 --> 00:10:54.435
And as you've probably seen, it
can hallucinate with its answers,

00:10:54.435 --> 00:10:57.336
and with accessibility, that comes
through in a lot of ways, like with

00:10:57.358 --> 00:10:59.929
alt text for images being inaccurate.

00:11:01.277 --> 00:11:06.005
Even more concerning is that
AI is actively excluding people

00:11:06.021 --> 00:11:07.343
in the disability community.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:14.209
Disabled voices are often left out of
the design of models and AI solutions.

00:11:14.876 --> 00:11:22.827
And most data that AI is trained
on is inaccessible, uh, in and of

00:11:22.827 --> 00:11:27.921
itself and lacks disability context.

00:11:28.423 --> 00:11:34.568
And on top of that, many AI tools
assume non-disabled defaults.

00:11:35.147 --> 00:11:38.824
Uh, and I'm not necessarily talking as
much about, you know, the Claude Codes

00:11:38.846 --> 00:11:44.814
and the ChatGPTs, but you have probably
seen that many, many, many companies are

00:11:44.839 --> 00:11:51.386
offering AI as a feature, um, for their,
um, products and services these days.

00:11:51.688 --> 00:11:55.155
And not all of them know about
accessibility and how to implement it

00:11:55.174 --> 00:12:01.352
well into their products and services,
let alone their AI-powered ones.

00:12:02.248 --> 00:12:09.194
So we are left to sort of deal with
this spectrum of usefulness versus

00:12:09.195 --> 00:12:13.277
challenging, um, situations here.

00:12:13.929 --> 00:12:20.311
And I wanna dig into what I talked about
with, um, AI generating inaccessible,

00:12:20.771 --> 00:12:23.492
uh, code and contect-- content.

00:12:23.493 --> 00:12:27.421
And this is unfortunately
because AI-assisted, uh, coding

00:12:27.421 --> 00:12:29.866
is inaccessible by default.

00:12:30.347 --> 00:12:34.420
And I wrote a blog about this,
um, uh, a few months ago.

00:12:34.424 --> 00:12:41.830
Basically, um Talking about how
because the internet is riddled

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:47.683
with an inaccessible code and
code examples, most large language

00:12:47.702 --> 00:12:50.392
models are learning from that.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:59.493
Um, Anna Cook wrote another post, um, sort
of calling this out, uh, w-with respect

00:12:59.532 --> 00:13:02.757
to the WebAIM Million report this year.

00:13:03.422 --> 00:13:09.004
Each year, AIM, uh, produces a report,
as you probably know, that, um, sort

00:13:09.005 --> 00:13:18.254
of talks about the top accessibility
errors on the, uh, top one million

00:13:18.266 --> 00:13:21.213
homepages a-across the, the internet.

00:13:21.715 --> 00:13:27.761
And they calculate how many
automatable, uh, defect detections

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:28.948
they find on these sites.

00:13:28.949 --> 00:13:33.704
And so for the past several years,
it's hovered between 95 and 97%

00:13:34.263 --> 00:13:35.648
and gone up and down a little bit.

00:13:36.018 --> 00:13:40.683
And you-- if you can see the graphing,
uh, on this page, you'll notice that

00:13:40.683 --> 00:13:47.520
there's a, uh, a purple line that
shows this percentage year after year.

00:13:48.043 --> 00:13:52.632
Now, what was concerning is
that it slowly had been making--

00:13:52.673 --> 00:13:57.292
th-this percentage had slowly been
declining over, uh, several years.

00:13:57.316 --> 00:14:00.534
But then in 2026, the report
actually showed an uptick.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:07.964
And the folks at w-AIM, uh, WebAIM,
they, they asserted that this

00:14:07.964 --> 00:14:14.408
happened, happened at the same time as
AI-assisted coding, uh, is exploding.

00:14:14.782 --> 00:14:19.339
And data backs that up, too, because if
you look at the Stack Overflow Developer

00:14:19.341 --> 00:14:25.553
Survey, it's showing that, um, over the
last few years, developers have adopted

00:14:25.577 --> 00:14:28.647
AI coding tools at an astounding rate.

00:14:29.305 --> 00:14:36.787
And as that rate of adoption converged
with , you know, with, uh, went towards

00:14:36.801 --> 00:14:46.299
85 and 90%, um, we saw the, um, we,
we saw the WebAIM report's percentage

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:48.505
go up again, which is unfortunate.

00:14:48.872 --> 00:14:52.934
Now, there may be other reasons for that,
and I wouldn't take this as hard, uh,

00:14:53.144 --> 00:14:57.388
data and research yet, but it's anecdotal
evidence that seems to be playing

00:14:57.389 --> 00:14:59.578
itself out in a lot of arenas here.

00:15:01.881 --> 00:15:02.954
So what can we do about it?

00:15:03.498 --> 00:15:07.611
I mean, we all know that AI is here
to stay, and many of us have found

00:15:07.612 --> 00:15:12.816
ways to make our lives more productive
and our work more efficient with it.

00:15:13.377 --> 00:15:18.335
Well, for me, I found several, um,
points of guidance and guardrails

00:15:18.335 --> 00:15:25.375
that I use, uh, to sort of help my
own work, um, with AI be as, um,

00:15:26.749 --> 00:15:32.084
as q- high quality, eh, as possible
and keep its integrity, if you will.

00:15:32.106 --> 00:15:34.226
And so I wanna spend like a
few minutes going over those.

00:15:35.365 --> 00:15:39.719
I found that AI works best
for me when I employ it on

00:15:39.719 --> 00:15:41.630
repetitive and structured tasks.

00:15:42.333 --> 00:15:48.198
So in the accessibility world, that
might be drafting reports or formatting

00:15:48.606 --> 00:15:54.930
notes, summarizing, uh, you know,
findings, uh, from automated tools,

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:59.370
or generating templates that I'm going
to use to send reports to clients.

00:16:00.026 --> 00:16:05.043
Um, it's really useful in early stage
brainstorming and iteration, uh, things

00:16:05.043 --> 00:16:10.856
like exploring ideas or UI concepts,
um, alt-- drafting alt texts, and

00:16:10.857 --> 00:16:15.151
then, um, remediation approaches,
you know, experimenting with those.

00:16:15.703 --> 00:16:17.923
Admin and operational work it's great for.

00:16:18.057 --> 00:16:25.187
F- uh, I use a lot of AI for, um, helping
customize follow-up emails or proposal

00:16:25.187 --> 00:16:28.980
scaffolding, um, internal documentation.

00:16:28.986 --> 00:16:31.626
And, and this is great because it
leaves more time for the work that

00:16:31.626 --> 00:16:35.046
I really wanna be doing, uh, helping
to make the web more accessible.

00:16:35.892 --> 00:16:40.593
And then, um, it's also great for
accelerating lower risk deliverables.

00:16:41.117 --> 00:16:45.774
Things like first drafts of, you know,
marketing copy or content, uh, training

00:16:45.774 --> 00:16:49.605
material that I'm going to go over,
like outlines and things like that, and

00:16:49.606 --> 00:16:56.432
then reports that I'm going to review
I don't use AI for certain things as

00:16:56.432 --> 00:16:59.716
much as I use it for certain things,
and I think this is just as critical.

00:17:00.184 --> 00:17:04.586
Um, I, I, I really don't think
it's a good idea to use AI for

00:17:04.597 --> 00:17:10.389
business-critical decisions, things
like accessibility strategy or legal

00:17:10.391 --> 00:17:16.887
positioning, uh, production launches,
uh, or high-impact recommendations.

00:17:17.084 --> 00:17:21.365
Um, you know, it may sort of inform
those a little bit, but I'm gonna,

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:27.458
I'm gonna use my expertise and my
perspective to really, uh, create

00:17:27.459 --> 00:17:29.735
those assets, make those decisions.

00:17:30.306 --> 00:17:33.543
I'm not gonna use it for
work that differentiates me.

00:17:34.115 --> 00:17:38.745
Um, you're, you're the only one with
your voice and your particular style and

00:17:38.746 --> 00:17:44.147
experience, and you don't wanna let AI
take that creativity away from you because

00:17:44.147 --> 00:17:47.626
that what's ma- what, what makes you, you.

00:17:48.368 --> 00:17:52.879
And it what's h- it, it's what helps you
build meaningful relationships with your

00:17:52.879 --> 00:17:54.976
clients and your partners around you.

00:17:56.439 --> 00:18:00.353
I try to avoid AI for
important human conversations.

00:18:00.758 --> 00:18:05.730
If I need to have a sensitive discussion
that might involve conflict or resolution,

00:18:06.293 --> 00:18:08.103
uh, I'm not gonna use AI for that.

00:18:08.202 --> 00:18:12.660
That, that to me is something that should
be person to person, human to human.

00:18:13.243 --> 00:18:17.963
Things like coaching and training,
uh, I also want to have those be,

00:18:18.369 --> 00:18:26.473
uh, real human-to-human interactions
because, um, I find that skills and,

00:18:26.473 --> 00:18:33.746
uh, expertise is caught as often as it
is taught, and AI really-- It's hard

00:18:33.747 --> 00:18:35.495
to catch things like that from AI.

00:18:36.712 --> 00:18:42.689
And then tasks requiring intuition
or, uh, deep niche experience

00:18:42.735 --> 00:18:47.567
and expertise are also sort of
questionable gray areas for AI.

00:18:47.971 --> 00:18:54.190
Things like con-complex usability
judgments or, uh, non-trivial workflows

00:18:54.504 --> 00:18:57.151
or, uh, deeply contextual decisions.

00:18:57.369 --> 00:19:00.624
Again, AI can inform and help with
these, but the-- at the end of the

00:19:00.625 --> 00:19:05.224
day, um, if I'm going to be responsible
for supporting and backing something

00:19:05.225 --> 00:19:08.844
up, I wanna make sure I made the
decisions around it, and I know why.

00:19:10.042 --> 00:19:13.135
And so over the last year, I've
actually come up with some really

00:19:13.137 --> 00:19:18.997
helpful rules, uh, for how to use
generate-- generative AI in my work.

00:19:20.447 --> 00:19:26.861
So firstly, um, I tend to be an
optimist, so I actively seek out new

00:19:26.866 --> 00:19:32.701
ways to experiment, uh, with AI in my
workflow and in my everyday activities.

00:19:33.244 --> 00:19:40.039
However, I'm only going to rely on AI
for tasks that I have experienced myself

00:19:40.226 --> 00:19:42.657
and am comfortable doing on my own.

00:19:43.916 --> 00:19:45.210
And the reason for that is simple: I

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:45.846
can't

00:19:46.488 --> 00:19:50.061
accurately review what I don't understand.

00:19:51.127 --> 00:19:54.627
And so if I d-- if I ask AI to do
something for me that I've never done,

00:19:55.460 --> 00:20:00.391
I'm not really gonna understand the how
behind it, and I'm always going to review

00:20:00.665 --> 00:20:06.146
AI-generated content for accuracy and
completeness because hallucinations are

00:20:06.146 --> 00:20:09.679
real, and AI speaks them very confidently.

00:20:10.726 --> 00:20:16.109
Whenever I get a-AI output, I'm going
to revise it, uh, so that it reflects

00:20:16.109 --> 00:20:19.437
my own expertise, tone, and judgment.

00:20:20.082 --> 00:20:24.143
Um, I'm-- I always wanna customize
it before sharing it or publishing

00:20:24.143 --> 00:20:30.520
with any-anybody because I need to
preserve, uh, my voice and my integrity.

00:20:31.012 --> 00:20:35.282
Uh, my reputation is, is, is
at stake here, and I don't want

00:20:35.293 --> 00:20:37.199
to hand that off to a machine.

00:20:39.104 --> 00:20:44.502
And I found that using detailed prompts
and conversational chats are very useful.

00:20:44.764 --> 00:20:49.211
When you're very specific with AI,
it tends to do better, and, um,

00:20:50.225 --> 00:20:51.639
you know, that's all fine and good.

00:20:52.296 --> 00:20:55.532
But I've also found that trying
to wrestle with AI too much on a

00:20:55.532 --> 00:20:57.161
problem can be counterproductive.

00:20:57.453 --> 00:21:02.979
And so I'll stop if the prompting
work becomes more challenging

00:21:02.979 --> 00:21:04.466
than the project itself.

00:21:05.006 --> 00:21:09.385
And in fact, I like to
regularly skip AI to stay sharp.

00:21:09.853 --> 00:21:14.551
Um, there's this phenomenon that's
been called situational disempowerment,

00:21:14.679 --> 00:21:19.800
where people are actually outsourcing
their value judgments, uh, and their

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:21.449
critical thinking skills to AI.

00:21:22.074 --> 00:21:28.040
Um, they are trusting AI with expertise
that they have, but in doing so, it sort

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:33.864
of whittles away at their own, um, edge,
and I need to be able to maintain that.

00:21:33.864 --> 00:21:37.433
So if AI goes away tomorrow, I
need to be able to do my job and

00:21:37.433 --> 00:21:38.806
to make those decisions well.

00:21:39.571 --> 00:21:45.071
So that's a little bit about how I use
AI and the guidelines that surround it.

00:21:45.606 --> 00:21:48.322
So now I want to just take a,
a really quick trip into some

00:21:48.326 --> 00:21:53.588
practical WordPress-based, uh,
applications for AI in accessibility.

00:21:54.553 --> 00:21:58.727
And these are gonna fall into a couple
different categories: uh, content creation

00:21:58.786 --> 00:22:02.064
and, uh, more technical developments.

00:22:02.322 --> 00:22:05.950
So for the content creation stuff, I
want us to start with, uh, thinking

00:22:05.951 --> 00:22:09.524
about ChatGPT, um, to generate copy.

00:22:09.524 --> 00:22:14.039
I found it to be pretty good with
content generation, and so I'll use

00:22:14.068 --> 00:22:19.077
ChatGPT a lot for drafting social
media posts or blog articles, uh,

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:24.469
newsletters, alternative text for
images, audio description scripts.

00:22:24.501 --> 00:22:27.769
I've u- I've, I've used ChatGPT
successfully for all of these.

00:22:28.463 --> 00:22:33.268
And digging down a little bit, um,
another type of content is images.

00:22:33.336 --> 00:22:38.857
Um, ChatGPT can actually help with
generating promotional banners or blog

00:22:38.858 --> 00:22:45.444
posts, um, uh, hero images, newsletter
graphics, uh, simple charts even.

00:22:46.152 --> 00:22:51.430
Um, one tool I will, I will plug
here is a website called Venngage.

00:22:51.451 --> 00:22:56.328
This is a platform, um, that can sort
of be considered as a Canva alternative.

00:22:56.699 --> 00:23:01.967
Um, it's a web-based design
system with a sharp focus on

00:23:02.049 --> 00:23:04.519
accessibility and AI tooling.

00:23:04.795 --> 00:23:10.556
So it'll generate images for you and
then help you to make sure their color

00:23:10.557 --> 00:23:16.231
contrast is, is, um, uh, correct and
that it gets good alt text for them.

00:23:16.579 --> 00:23:20.818
When you're generating content, it'll,
it'll, um, help you with heading

00:23:20.819 --> 00:23:27.592
orders and making sure that, um,
visual and reading orders, uh, match.

00:23:28.765 --> 00:23:32.105
And then, um, I've come up with this--
I've recently come up with this,

00:23:32.341 --> 00:23:39.036
this nice little template for helping
AI, um, generate alt text for me.

00:23:39.036 --> 00:23:42.748
I call it guided alt text
generation, and I'll talk about

00:23:42.749 --> 00:23:44.242
that more in a, in a moment.

00:23:45.179 --> 00:23:49.828
Um, AI is also great with
creating and using templates.

00:23:50.252 --> 00:23:54.850
So, um, different AI providers
have different names for these.

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:56.554
It could be skills or plugins.

00:23:57.827 --> 00:24:03.257
Um, uh, there are other names out there,
but essentially, uh, for instance, I have

00:24:03.258 --> 00:24:10.107
a template for when I am, um, writing my
newsletter, my regular newsletter, that

00:24:10.223 --> 00:24:13.627
understands its structure and what's
it supposed to-- what's supposed to be

00:24:13.628 --> 00:24:18.753
in it, and so I can give it, um, the
tools or, or, or the content I want it

00:24:18.756 --> 00:24:24.134
to describe, uh, in bullet points as
opposed to, you know, writing things out.

00:24:24.136 --> 00:24:29.654
So if I have a list of, uh, really
cool articles I wanna share about

00:24:29.791 --> 00:24:32.660
accessibility, I can give tho--
the URLs to those, and it'll

00:24:32.664 --> 00:24:34.147
find the title and the author.

00:24:34.405 --> 00:24:38.976
It can summarize the post, um,
and make it sort of a teaser that

00:24:38.976 --> 00:24:40.997
I can then edit and go through.

00:24:41.503 --> 00:24:43.064
Um, so that's neat.

00:24:43.721 --> 00:24:47.981
Uh, I've been able to make a
skill for project documentation

00:24:47.982 --> 00:24:49.122
or knowledge graphing.

00:24:49.636 --> 00:24:54.756
This is pretty cool because as I'm
developing, um, these days, you know,

00:24:54.756 --> 00:24:59.609
any sort of project, requirements
tend to shift and change as decisions

00:24:59.613 --> 00:25:01.217
are made and questions are asked.

00:25:01.697 --> 00:25:07.453
And I'm finding that if I'm, if I'm
working with an AI agent, uh, on

00:25:07.498 --> 00:25:13.262
code, um, it-- there are ways to
help it understand what's changing

00:25:13.263 --> 00:25:15.695
and document that as we go along.

00:25:16.168 --> 00:25:19.647
And that's really helpful because
it saves a lot of time and,

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:22.320
and review of, uh, documents.

00:25:22.321 --> 00:25:27.013
I mean, obviously I do review the
documentation, but it's, it's neat that

00:25:27.014 --> 00:25:35.335
it's able to sort of just flow with the,
the work, uh, much more than in years gone

00:25:35.335 --> 00:25:37.190
by, where it was sort of a separate step.

00:25:38.416 --> 00:25:43.762
And then, uh, one really practical way
is, uh, for AI I found is that, you know,

00:25:43.763 --> 00:25:48.793
if I've got a social media post idea I
want to publish to several platforms,

00:25:49.186 --> 00:25:53.857
AI can help me tweak its voice and
language to optimize for those platforms.

00:25:53.869 --> 00:25:57.806
So an Instagram post is probably
gonna sound a little bit differently

00:25:58.122 --> 00:26:02.561
than a LinkedIn post, and AI can
help with that so that I can focus

00:26:02.581 --> 00:26:07.032
on the core message and let it
handle the specifics of platforms.

00:26:08.539 --> 00:26:10.239
So a couple of examples here.

00:26:10.371 --> 00:26:15.527
Um, I wrote an article a little bit
ago about this process I'm using called

00:26:15.542 --> 00:26:22.572
guided alt text generation, and it
essentially te- asks three questions:

00:26:22.751 --> 00:26:29.567
what, where, and why about an image,
um, which will help surface the idea of

00:26:29.839 --> 00:26:34.202
the content of the image, the context
of the image, what's around it, and the

00:26:34.205 --> 00:26:36.589
purpose of the image, or why is it there?

00:26:37.037 --> 00:26:40.632
It turns out there are three
prepositions that go really nicely

00:26:40.633 --> 00:26:43.883
with each of those, um, terms.

00:26:44.139 --> 00:26:48.677
Uh, when you're asking what an
image is about, what is it of

00:26:48.707 --> 00:26:50.813
is a great way to, to put that.

00:26:51.317 --> 00:26:57.819
Uh, the context of image or where it is,
is-- can be, uh, captured in about, and

00:26:57.819 --> 00:27:00.957
then the purpose is why or what's it for?

00:27:01.535 --> 00:27:08.425
And so I found that using a prompt
that combines those three prepositions

00:27:08.675 --> 00:27:10.800
leads to pretty cool alt texts.

00:27:11.134 --> 00:27:16.077
So basically, I'll say something like,
"Give me alt text for this image of…"

00:27:16.683 --> 00:27:21.877
And then I say what the image is, "for,"
and then kind of describe the purpose

00:27:21.879 --> 00:27:25.693
of the image, "about," and then I'll
talk about the context of the image.

00:27:25.987 --> 00:27:28.695
So I just wanna show you
a quick example of that.

00:27:30.418 --> 00:27:30.709
And

00:27:35.077 --> 00:27:42.867
So here I am at ChatGPT right now and I'm
going to upload an image from my computer

00:27:47.681 --> 00:27:48.819
Just called image.

00:27:49.909 --> 00:27:54.167
And, you know, I, I've seen so many
people using AI just say something like,

00:27:54.568 --> 00:28:00.865
"Generate alt text for this image." And
so it thinks a bit, and then it comes back

00:28:00.866 --> 00:28:03.956
and says, "Okay, uh, here's the alt text.

00:28:03.956 --> 00:28:09.608
Students re- students relax on a grassy
college campus in front of historic

00:28:09.608 --> 00:28:15.439
red brick buildings with leaf, uh,
leafless trees, outdoor chairs, and

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:21.547
paved, uh, and paved walkways, uh,
under a par- a partly cloudy sky."

00:28:21.764 --> 00:28:22.171
Okay.

00:28:22.352 --> 00:28:23.427
That's actually true.

00:28:23.511 --> 00:28:25.729
If you can see the image,
it sorta looks like that.

00:28:26.612 --> 00:28:30.099
But that's for… And, and I mean, that's
not bad alt text, to be honest with

00:28:30.100 --> 00:28:32.585
you, but it's not very focused either.

00:28:33.288 --> 00:28:38.086
Um, if I can ask the… If I can
add some details for this using

00:28:38.088 --> 00:28:41.711
that template, we might get a
l- a lot more specific alt text.

00:28:41.944 --> 00:28:51.074
So say I was, uh, generating alt
text for this image of, um, and the

00:28:51.074 --> 00:28:54.956
target a- the target of this was
going to be an article about famous,

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:59.631
uh, residents of this dormitory
here, which is called Hollis Hall.

00:29:00.257 --> 00:29:05.017
So then I would say something
like, "Generate alt text, uh,

00:29:05.017 --> 00:29:06.518
of this image of Hollis Hall

00:29:09.087 --> 00:29:23.229
for an article titled Famous
Residents of Hollis Hall about

00:29:24.101 --> 00:29:35.402
noteworthy, uh, misspelled it,
noteworthy occupant of the dorm.

00:29:37.569 --> 00:29:40.860
We give that to ChatGPT, and
it'll come back hopefully with

00:29:40.861 --> 00:29:43.662
something a little more helpful.

00:29:45.146 --> 00:29:45.607
Here we go.

00:29:46.915 --> 00:29:54.166
It says, "Hollis Hall, a historic red
brick Harvard dormitory surrounded by a

00:29:54.166 --> 00:30:01.408
leafless, uh, and grassy, uh, courtyard
where students sit and work on a sunny

00:30:01.408 --> 00:30:06.608
day." So I wouldn't say that's perfect,
but it's more in the right direction

00:30:06.610 --> 00:30:12.984
because you'll see the, uh, focus of
the alt text now is on the subject

00:30:12.984 --> 00:30:18.607
of the article that we're, that we
are, um, going to, um, place this in.

00:30:19.121 --> 00:30:21.733
And so again, I'm not gonna
use that alt text as is.

00:30:21.741 --> 00:30:26.371
I'm probably gonna look at it and re--
and, and, uh, review it more, but you can

00:30:26.371 --> 00:30:28.301
see that it's, it's much more on target.

00:30:29.309 --> 00:30:30.703
Okay, moving on

00:30:33.701 --> 00:30:37.868
I also wanna talk about how, uh,
I've been able to use AI, um,

00:30:37.882 --> 00:30:40.021
to generate accessible images.

00:30:40.419 --> 00:30:44.661
Um, a-and ChatGPT's gotten
fairly good at this.

00:30:45.103 --> 00:30:49.961
Um, a few months ago, I was helping
plan a, um, an event that was

00:30:49.967 --> 00:30:55.596
bringing, uh, disabled storytellers
together to share, uh, tales of

00:30:55.638 --> 00:30:57.744
our challenges and adventures.

00:30:57.774 --> 00:31:02.010
And, um, this is part of a,
an organization I, I help out

00:31:02.011 --> 00:31:05.240
with called Disabled Stories
Collective, or DisCo for short.

00:31:05.773 --> 00:31:09.237
And, uh, this was going to
be our first online event.

00:31:09.526 --> 00:31:14.639
And so the, um, organizer sent me
an email with details, including the

00:31:14.639 --> 00:31:21.088
date, time, location, and the theme,
and I was able to use ChatGPT, like,

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:23.680
uh, like this to generate an image.

00:31:23.721 --> 00:31:28.275
I just, I, I started with a simple
prompt that said, "Design a banner for

00:31:28.275 --> 00:31:33.232
a disabled storytellers event using
info from this email." And then I just

00:31:33.241 --> 00:31:35.323
pasted the contents of the email in.

00:31:35.941 --> 00:31:38.775
And it came back with its first
draft, which wasn't totally great.

00:31:38.789 --> 00:31:40.276
I won't show that right now.

00:31:40.696 --> 00:31:43.815
Um, but it was… there was a lot going
on in it, and said-- I, I essentially

00:31:43.815 --> 00:31:48.865
said, "It's too busy. Can you please
simplify it for me?" And then it

00:31:48.866 --> 00:31:53.950
came back and the colors weren't
really that, that distinguishable.

00:31:54.242 --> 00:31:55.881
And I said, "Okay, this
needs to be accessible.

00:31:56.132 --> 00:31:59.790
Please meet minimum contrast,
uh, requirements with your color

00:31:59.790 --> 00:32:02.688
combinations." And so then it
went away and worked and came back

00:32:02.688 --> 00:32:05.488
with something else, and it was
getting in the right way, okay.

00:32:05.495 --> 00:32:08.993
And I said, "Well, you know, I
have these logos, one for Disabled

00:32:08.998 --> 00:32:12.313
cl- Disabled Stories Collective
and another organization that

00:32:12.314 --> 00:32:13.452
we're working with w- for this."

00:32:13.467 --> 00:32:14.802
It was the Perkins Library.

00:32:15.253 --> 00:32:22.038
Um, and so then put those into it and…
But, um, the contrast, the text that

00:32:22.038 --> 00:32:24.798
came back was again inaccessible, so
I said, "Oh, wait a minute, you gotta

00:32:24.798 --> 00:32:26.878
make that accessible." So it did that.

00:32:26.897 --> 00:32:30.642
And so finally, uh, some of the
taglines and stuff came back, but they

00:32:30.642 --> 00:32:35.267
were a little verbose, and so I said,
"Shorten this teaser," uh, essentially.

00:32:35.426 --> 00:32:37.053
Uh, the prompts were a little
longer than this, but you

00:32:37.055 --> 00:32:38.306
get, you get the general idea.

00:32:38.358 --> 00:32:42.766
But after these six prompts, it
did a fairly good job putting

00:32:42.767 --> 00:32:46.543
together, um, an accessible banner.

00:32:46.685 --> 00:32:53.433
Um, here on the screen now is that
banner with the DISCO and Perkins

00:32:53.434 --> 00:33:01.579
Library, um- logos at the top, um, in
front of a, a light green background,

00:33:01.579 --> 00:33:06.529
and then beneath that it says Virtually
Able: Stories of Disabled Tech Life,

00:33:06.571 --> 00:33:10.890
um, on-- written in dark black and
dark green on top of a light green.

00:33:11.192 --> 00:33:15.765
Next to them, you've got icons of a
computer and a phone with, you know, the

00:33:15.767 --> 00:33:17.950
universal accessibility symbol and stuff.

00:33:18.317 --> 00:33:26.876
And then the tagline for the event, uh,
Real Stories, Tech We Use, Lives We Share,

00:33:26.923 --> 00:33:33.901
again, in that dark black and, uh, green,
uh, colors on the, on the light green hue.

00:33:34.235 --> 00:33:35.218
It meets color contrast.

00:33:35.221 --> 00:33:40.065
And below that you've got the, uh,
specifics of the events, uh, with

00:33:40.084 --> 00:33:41.442
white text on black background.

00:33:41.449 --> 00:33:45.104
Friday, May 29th at 7:00 PM, virtual.

00:33:45.378 --> 00:33:48.986
And so we were able to post this on
social media and share it with folks.

00:33:49.013 --> 00:33:51.064
Um, it worked pretty well, and
it didn't really take that long.

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:51.956
So that was neat.

00:33:53.723 --> 00:34:01.223
Accessibility fixes, um, uh, are often
hard to find and more chal-- even

00:34:01.223 --> 00:34:04.518
more challenging to, uh, remediate.

00:34:04.843 --> 00:34:09.037
Uh, but I'm finding that with Claude
Code, some of my development work,

00:34:09.103 --> 00:34:10.877
uh, can be sped up with those.

00:34:11.313 --> 00:34:16.558
Um, AI-assisted coding, uh, I'm finding
for accessibility works best when you

00:34:16.563 --> 00:34:23.723
have a specific and bounded, um, problem
that-- whose fixes you can review well.

00:34:24.228 --> 00:34:28.619
Um, and Claude Code specifically, I
found works directly in a local Git

00:34:28.621 --> 00:34:35.375
repository or project folder, uh, on
your machine with existing, uh, you

00:34:35.376 --> 00:34:40.973
know, a theme plugin, uh, architecture
that your WordPress, uh, site may have.

00:34:41.853 --> 00:34:46.752
So, um, it's a strong fit I found for
targeted accessibility improvements

00:34:46.752 --> 00:34:52.097
like keyboard operations, semantic
markup or ARIA, uh, focus and

00:34:52.112 --> 00:34:54.286
interaction, interaction behavior.

00:34:54.827 --> 00:35:01.286
But again, this requires that human
expertise, uh, still owns the outcome.

00:35:02.226 --> 00:35:06.549
I need to define the issue, I need
to review the code, and I need to

00:35:06.549 --> 00:35:10.564
test with keyboard and assistive
technology and deploy intentionally,

00:35:10.734 --> 00:35:12.756
not just letting Claude Code do that.

00:35:14.221 --> 00:35:16.021
So here's a fun example I, I had.

00:35:16.063 --> 00:35:22.184
Um, I have a, a client, uh, that, like
many other, um, companies, wants to

00:35:22.185 --> 00:35:28.159
have sort of a, a background video
on their homepage hero image and,

00:35:28.178 --> 00:35:30.085
uh, or their homepage hero area.

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:35.916
And as we may or may not know,
that's, uh, often a thing that, that,

00:35:35.951 --> 00:35:41.469
uh, clients do that's inaccessible
because, um, it needs to have a

00:35:41.479 --> 00:35:43.920
pause or play button control with it.

00:35:44.046 --> 00:35:47.833
And, uh, most widgets out
of the box don't do that.

00:35:47.953 --> 00:35:53.593
So, uh, for this issue, um, this,
this video was already there, and I

00:35:53.593 --> 00:35:54.822
needed to make it more accessible.

00:35:54.825 --> 00:36:00.214
So my goal was to give users
control, uh, over motion without

00:36:00.289 --> 00:36:02.777
disrupting the visual design.

00:36:03.235 --> 00:36:09.608
So Claude Code was able to update,
um, the custom theme that this

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:10.883
WordPress site was built on.

00:36:11.570 --> 00:36:14.640
Before this, it's funny, th-this
custom theme had kind of been kind

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:18.562
of over-engineered, but the fact that
it was already in Git and in a repo

00:36:18.568 --> 00:36:23.557
made it, uh, fairly straightforward
to, uh, give to Claude, and then it

00:36:23.591 --> 00:36:28.677
would straddle the, the theme and fix
the PHP, JavaScript, and CSS for that.

00:36:29.553 --> 00:36:33.659
Um, and it actually made the change
in the video plugin component itself,

00:36:33.661 --> 00:36:35.894
so it was-- it had a site-wide impact.

00:36:36.818 --> 00:36:41.414
Now again, human review was needed
to verify that keyboard operation and

00:36:41.415 --> 00:36:45.431
screen reader usage, uh, and the, and
that the announcements worked properly.

00:36:47.515 --> 00:36:52.018
The key lesson here is that AI can
write, uh, the patch, but I still

00:36:52.018 --> 00:36:54.393
need to know what fixed means.

00:36:54.716 --> 00:37:01.479
And I'll show you that really quickly
here, um, because… So here, this is,

00:37:01.488 --> 00:37:03.361
this is kind of the, the website here.

00:37:03.362 --> 00:37:07.083
You can see the video playing in
the background behind the text here.

00:37:07.634 --> 00:37:11.625
And then in the back, uh, at
the edge of the, the video here,

00:37:11.627 --> 00:37:15.306
you've, you-- on the bottom right,
you've got this pause button.

00:37:15.715 --> 00:37:18.721
And so I can use my mouse
to pause that or to play it.

00:37:20.018 --> 00:37:24.800
And in fact, um, that control
is keyboard focusable.

00:37:24.808 --> 00:37:26.888
I'm not sure if you can
see, but my mouse, my…

00:37:26.974 --> 00:37:28.586
I'm tabbing around the controls.

00:37:28.791 --> 00:37:32.425
Now I'm on the control,
and I hit Enter to stop it.

00:37:32.425 --> 00:37:34.672
I can press space bar to start it again.

00:37:35.375 --> 00:37:36.701
Screen reader works as well.

00:37:36.701 --> 00:37:37.797
I'm not gonna demonstrate that now.

00:37:37.797 --> 00:37:44.095
But I was able to go a little bit even
further and have the AI make it so that if

00:37:44.169 --> 00:37:48.999
tapping or clicking anywhere on the image
itself or the video itself would, would

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:54.269
pause it, which was really helpful, um,
and is a little bit beyond what the WCAG

00:37:54.310 --> 00:37:56.794
calls for, but very useful none the same.

00:37:58.404 --> 00:37:59.800
All right, finishing up here.

00:38:00.716 --> 00:38:07.261
I wanna talk a little bit about WordPress,
um, site building, uh, with accessibility

00:38:07.261 --> 00:38:10.277
in mind, um, using Claude Code.

00:38:10.293 --> 00:38:12.277
'Cause this is something
I'm just getting into now.

00:38:12.614 --> 00:38:19.384
But the goal might be to rebuild an
existing client site from scratch with

00:38:19.432 --> 00:38:25.723
WCAG 2.2 AA as an explicit requirement
and target from the beginning.

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:31.905
So, um, I had this client who had an
existing website and wanted to redo

00:38:31.906 --> 00:38:35.317
it because it had some security, some
performance, some accessibility issues.

00:38:35.912 --> 00:38:39.818
And what I was able to do is just
ask Claude to scrape that site and

00:38:39.959 --> 00:38:46.633
generate an entirely new WordPress,
um, installation of it, uh, keeping

00:38:46.643 --> 00:38:52.398
the content and the general, uh,
structure of the, of the site intact.

00:38:52.435 --> 00:38:57.189
But updating the UI such that
it was much more accessible and

00:38:57.189 --> 00:38:59.135
user-friendly after doing that.

00:38:59.599 --> 00:39:02.072
I specified an accessible theme to use.

00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:08.300
I told it to look at WCAG for this, and,
um, it set up a local installation with

00:39:08.309 --> 00:39:14.150
Docker and wp-env, and actually set up
a, a custom startup plugin where it could

00:39:14.152 --> 00:39:17.151
run PHP to build the site from scratch.

00:39:18.579 --> 00:39:21.892
So after doing this, we did
some testing on, on the website.

00:39:21.972 --> 00:39:27.012
Uh, it went largely well, but
again, uh, AI isn't perfect, and

00:39:27.072 --> 00:39:30.205
it thought it had made st- certain
things accessible, but it hadn't.

00:39:30.553 --> 00:39:34.671
And when I went to test the FAQ
page, I realized that the AI

00:39:34.681 --> 00:39:40.310
had had a, had a lot of trouble,
um, capturing and communicating

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:42.925
whe-- the state of each FAQ item.

00:39:43.363 --> 00:39:49.504
Okay, on the screen, you'll see an image
that shows a short list of FAQ items all

00:39:49.504 --> 00:39:54.558
closed, which is visually indicated by
a right-pointing triangle to their left.

00:39:55.231 --> 00:39:58.847
So that shows you visually whether
they're closed or open, but underneath,

00:39:59.516 --> 00:40:04.229
um, the HTML uses ARIA to distinguish
this, so screen reader users know

00:40:04.229 --> 00:40:05.314
whether they're open or closed.

00:40:05.575 --> 00:40:08.199
And the AI had a lot of
problems getting this right.

00:40:08.199 --> 00:40:12.665
We had to go back and forth to make
sure the state, uh, ARIA expanded

00:40:12.668 --> 00:40:18.379
or ARIA collapsed was communicated
properly, and, um, to make sure

00:40:18.379 --> 00:40:19.538
it was announced correctly.

00:40:20.127 --> 00:40:24.750
And so it just went to show that even
though the code and the visuals can

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:32.033
look good, uh, we still need to, uh,
test with AT 'cause it could fail there

00:40:32.051 --> 00:40:34.071
even after the AI thought it was done.

00:40:35.466 --> 00:40:39.151
And so finally, I just wanna mention in
quick passing that there's this fairly

00:40:39.152 --> 00:40:44.625
new idea called MCP, which stands for
Model Cont- Model Context Protocol, which

00:40:44.645 --> 00:40:52.033
essentially lets AI tools interact with
external systems based on, um, permissions

00:40:52.039 --> 00:40:57.400
and authentications and, uh, whatever
exposed features are available to it.

00:40:58.305 --> 00:40:59.408
This is coming to WordPress.

00:40:59.413 --> 00:41:03.691
It's already there in a lot
of ways because, um, WordPress

00:41:04.150 --> 00:41:07.146
Core released an MCP adapter.

00:41:07.613 --> 00:41:13.089
And from what I can see, that's mostly
being used right now for reporting

00:41:13.107 --> 00:41:16.155
and, um, read-only type of operations.

00:41:16.181 --> 00:41:21.860
Although plugins like WooCommerce
have, uh, begun delving into

00:41:21.895 --> 00:41:24.688
product inventory management with
it, which is really powerful.

00:41:24.927 --> 00:41:28.495
But this essentially lets you do on
a live website what I was just sort

00:41:28.495 --> 00:41:30.109
of talking about on my local machine.

00:41:30.937 --> 00:41:36.995
And this means that what's coming
is more powerful AI agents that

00:41:36.997 --> 00:41:42.096
facilitate accessibility, accessibility
fixes with a single click.

00:41:42.609 --> 00:41:46.135
And I know this is coming
because, uh, companies like

00:41:46.142 --> 00:41:51.552
Deque are adding MCP servers to
do just that to their offerings.

00:41:51.967 --> 00:41:55.364
And maybe, you know, we're
recording this a little bit before

00:41:55.365 --> 00:41:58.358
the actual WP Accessibility Day.

00:41:58.388 --> 00:41:59.630
Maybe it's there by now.

00:41:59.985 --> 00:42:03.023
But I just wanted to put that on your
minds 'cause it-- this is a tool with a

00:42:03.024 --> 00:42:05.541
lot of potential and a lot of challenge.

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:11.644
So just to wrap up, my main point
here again is to use AI to accelerate

00:42:11.651 --> 00:42:17.350
scaffolding and support work, and
use human experience and judgment

00:42:17.397 --> 00:42:19.755
to ensure accessibility is there.

00:42:20.760 --> 00:42:24.267
Because AI, as we've talked
about simul-simultaneously

00:42:24.274 --> 00:42:28.150
helps, harms, and excludes users.

00:42:28.762 --> 00:42:33.336
Uh, and so we need to use it
intentionally and not just automatically.

00:42:34.231 --> 00:42:38.087
We want-- We need to verify everything
it outputs because it will respond

00:42:38.091 --> 00:42:41.299
confidently whether it's right or wrong.

00:42:42.489 --> 00:42:47.004
And on the plus side, there are always
new tools coming out, and so we wanna

00:42:47.005 --> 00:42:53.049
find tools and workflows that fit our
context, our clients, and our style.

00:42:53.839 --> 00:42:58.216
And again, I wanna just end with this
point because it's central, is I feel

00:42:58.217 --> 00:43:03.745
like accessibility expertise matters
more than ever today in AI-driven work

00:43:03.751 --> 00:43:08.052
because we need to be able to tell
AI to be accessible and then evaluate

00:43:08.052 --> 00:43:10.106
whether it is or is not accessible.

00:43:11.278 --> 00:43:12.611
So thank you so much for listening.

00:43:12.712 --> 00:43:16.845
I have, um, information on here
if you wanna get in touch with me.

00:43:17.240 --> 00:43:18.968
Uh, again, my name is Simon Miner.

00:43:18.968 --> 00:43:21.514
My website is pedalpoint.com.

00:43:22.127 --> 00:43:25.799
Uh, my email, LinkedIn, and
newsletters are up there.

00:43:25.809 --> 00:43:30.996
I'll let you look at the downloaded slides
for, um, specifics on those if you want.

00:43:31.343 --> 00:43:34.297
But I thank you so much, and
I wish you all a great day.

00:43:38.706 --> 00:43:41.350
Joe: Thank you so much, Simon,
for that great presentation.

00:43:42.095 --> 00:43:46.943
Um, there's quite a few questions
in the Q&A, but I wanted to,

00:43:46.973 --> 00:43:48.411
to start you off with this.

00:43:48.744 --> 00:43:54.467
Um, I love how you described how you
were learning and experimenting with AI.

00:43:54.803 --> 00:43:59.598
Uh, which AI tools do you prefer, and do
you prefer them f- for specific tasks?

00:44:01.520 --> 00:44:02.907
Simon: So that's a great question.

00:44:02.947 --> 00:44:06.863
Um, so I primarily use, uh,
Claude for development work.

00:44:07.375 --> 00:44:11.798
Um, it does a really good job
sitting on top of GitHub repos

00:44:12.110 --> 00:44:15.089
and, um, uh, working with them.

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:21.499
Uh, for more creative or design
things, I find that OpenAI, uh,

00:44:21.548 --> 00:44:24.125
tends to be, um, a little better.

00:44:24.125 --> 00:44:25.083
Now, it's interesting.

00:44:25.332 --> 00:44:33.761
Accessibility-wise, um, the ChatGPT
app until recently has been far

00:44:33.764 --> 00:44:38.006
and away better than Claude, but
then Claude Code has its thing.

00:44:38.006 --> 00:44:40.181
So, so it depends on what
you're looking for, kind of.

00:44:40.601 --> 00:44:40.971
Joe: Okay.

00:44:41.351 --> 00:44:42.337
Simon: That's kind of a short answer.

00:44:42.586 --> 00:44:47.027
Joe: Well, I, I like that because
Amy has sort of the opposite end,

00:44:47.119 --> 00:44:48.424
uh, in terms of her question.

00:44:48.757 --> 00:44:48.787
Oh, yeah?

00:44:48.787 --> 00:44:53.005
She said, "If you had to give up
AI for everything except one of the

00:44:53.005 --> 00:44:56.717
tasks that you discussed in your
presentation, which one would you…

00:44:57.045 --> 00:45:00.451
which one are you going to keep?"
And I'm gonna throw in, "And why?"

00:45:02.196 --> 00:45:02.621
Simon: Yeah.

00:45:02.622 --> 00:45:03.247
That's great.

00:45:03.346 --> 00:45:07.365
Um, so I'm gonna keep it
for the thing that I am most

00:45:07.379 --> 00:45:10.024
confident in doing on my own.

00:45:10.472 --> 00:45:15.216
Um, so in this case I would probably say
accessibility development and remediation,

00:45:15.243 --> 00:45:16.501
'cause I've been doing that for a while.

00:45:17.137 --> 00:45:22.299
I wouldn't trust AI, um, with something
I don't understand quite as well.

00:45:24.291 --> 00:45:24.685
Joe: Okay.

00:45:25.210 --> 00:45:26.477
All right, next question.

00:45:26.834 --> 00:45:29.979
Uh, do you feel descriptive
prompts resolve the bulk

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:32.054
of AI hallucination issues?

00:45:33.067 --> 00:45:37.062
And then secondarily, and
how about the images of folks

00:45:37.063 --> 00:45:38.744
with extra arms and fingers?

00:45:38.854 --> 00:45:40.138
Is there a solution for that?

00:45:42.131 --> 00:45:44.847
Simon: So, uh, descriptive
prompts are helpful.

00:45:45.316 --> 00:45:49.096
Again, um, it just… I- i- if
you're, if you've got some expertise

00:45:49.097 --> 00:45:50.835
on it, they tend to work better.

00:45:51.195 --> 00:45:56.351
Uh, extra arms and fingers, your
mileage may vary depending on whether

00:45:56.351 --> 00:46:00.804
you prompted for them or not, or,
um, more accurately, uh, prompted,

00:46:01.130 --> 00:46:02.903
explicitly prompted not to have them.

00:46:05.273 --> 00:46:05.565
Joe: Yeah.

00:46:05.719 --> 00:46:07.402
That's making my skin crawl just-

00:46:07.424 --> 00:46:08.109
Simon: I know, right?

00:46:08.288 --> 00:46:08.950
Joe: Oh, geez.

00:46:09.045 --> 00:46:09.327
Ooh.

00:46:09.327 --> 00:46:09.618
All right.

00:46:09.649 --> 00:46:14.158
Um, um, and, you know, during your
presentation, uh, you did a lot of

00:46:14.158 --> 00:46:16.554
great work in describing, uh, AI.

00:46:16.787 --> 00:46:21.651
Um, in describing AI's function and
scope, um, I've heard it referred

00:46:21.652 --> 00:46:24.186
to as a pencil, uh, nothing more.

00:46:24.349 --> 00:46:27.351
How would you describe it, and
more specifically in terms of how

00:46:27.351 --> 00:46:29.589
Pedal Point solution leverages it?

00:46:30.599 --> 00:46:31.663
Simon: Oh, goodness.

00:46:32.216 --> 00:46:37.281
Um, well, I think we aim to use
it as a pencil in a lot of our

00:46:37.281 --> 00:46:38.994
things, because it can be specific.

00:46:39.630 --> 00:46:43.364
Uh, if I'm honest, there have been times
I've mistakenly used it as a sledgehammer,

00:46:43.610 --> 00:46:46.179
and it hasn't worked that, that great.

00:46:46.761 --> 00:46:52.124
Uh, or as, like, a very broad fishing
net, which can bring up, uh, good

00:46:52.125 --> 00:46:55.113
things and bad things, and it's
up to you to sift through them.

00:46:55.719 --> 00:46:55.956
Okay.

00:46:55.956 --> 00:46:57.810
Um, so we're still learning with that.

00:46:58.237 --> 00:46:58.785
Joe: Oh, perfect.

00:46:58.811 --> 00:46:59.211
Perfect.

00:46:59.579 --> 00:47:01.432
The old bull in the China shop routine.

00:47:01.776 --> 00:47:02.868
Simon: Yes, exactly.

00:47:03.197 --> 00:47:06.916
Joe: Is there a way to use AI to
