00:47:08.606 --> 00:47:10.962
Simon: Yeah, I'm, I'm… Yes, absolutely.

00:47:11.103 --> 00:47:13.525
Um, in fact, I just
did that with a client.

00:47:13.864 --> 00:47:20.253
Um, they wanted their header navigation,
uh, which had, uh, submenus, um, to work

00:47:20.253 --> 00:47:26.813
with up and down, um, arrows in addition
to with, with Tab and Escape and all that.

00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:30.694
And, you know, I was like, "Well,
that's kind of… That might be a little

00:47:30.694 --> 00:47:32.882
advanced," but they really wanted it,
and there wasn't anything against it,

00:47:32.882 --> 00:47:39.661
so I just had, um, AI write a little
snippet for, um, W- the WPCode extension,

00:47:40.046 --> 00:47:45.568
um, that sat on top of, um, the theme
GeneratePress and, uh, did that.

00:47:45.569 --> 00:47:46.782
So you can absolutely do that.

00:47:47.234 --> 00:47:47.618
Joe: Awesome.

00:47:47.618 --> 00:47:47.628
Awesome.

00:47:47.628 --> 00:47:48.713
Simon: They're just event listeners.

00:47:48.797 --> 00:47:49.006
Yeah.

00:47:49.447 --> 00:47:51.026
Joe: Uh, Alice had a second question.

00:47:51.387 --> 00:47:55.272
Um, if you are beginning a
site from scratch, what do you

00:47:55.273 --> 00:47:58.198
recommend in using Claude Code?

00:47:59.352 --> 00:48:00.070
Simon: Hmm.

00:48:00.547 --> 00:48:02.766
So it depends on the nature of the site.

00:48:03.181 --> 00:48:08.071
I think if you've got a good design
in mind, wireframes, uh, pictures,

00:48:08.579 --> 00:48:12.362
um, sort of a good information
architecture, you could feed it that,

00:48:12.864 --> 00:48:16.697
and then it will sort of paint it
in broad strokes, in my experience.

00:48:16.968 --> 00:48:22.207
But you're always going to have
to hone it and, um, you know,

00:48:22.275 --> 00:48:25.851
uh, w- get, get to where it's a
pencil, you're using a pencil.

00:48:26.153 --> 00:48:29.590
And that can be… AI
can help with that, too.

00:48:29.873 --> 00:48:33.574
Sometimes it's just faster to do it
yourself, though, uh, when you get

00:48:33.574 --> 00:48:37.640
down to the, you know, uh, specific
content and things like that.

00:48:38.419 --> 00:48:40.576
Joe: What, what part of your
learning curve would you say you're,

00:48:40.723 --> 00:48:42.704
you're in in terms of learning AI?

00:48:44.013 --> 00:48:48.850
Simon: Uh, I would say I'm solidly
intermediate, uh, as long as they don't

00:48:48.850 --> 00:48:50.181
change it that much, which they do.

00:48:50.183 --> 00:48:53.024
So I'm probably
advanced-beginner-intermediate

00:48:53.326 --> 00:48:54.109
if I'm sprinting.

00:48:55.128 --> 00:48:55.601
Joe: All right.

00:48:55.895 --> 00:48:58.910
Okay, here's one more question with the
amount of time- Yeah … we have left.

00:48:59.179 --> 00:48:59.395
Sure.

00:48:59.395 --> 00:49:03.830
Uh, how can, how can AI be used
to retrospectively fix a website

00:49:03.845 --> 00:49:06.000
to be accessible after it's live?

00:49:06.965 --> 00:49:08.124
Simon: Uh, that's a great question.

00:49:08.462 --> 00:49:11.150
Um, first you wanna have a staging
environment or a development

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:15.185
environment, absolutely, before you
make any changes to a live, um, site.

00:49:15.755 --> 00:49:22.310
Then, um, you need the source or access
to the source, um, and the content.

00:49:22.643 --> 00:49:25.567
Um, I find it best if it's
checked into a Git repo.

00:49:25.899 --> 00:49:31.817
Um, then you can, uh, stack Claude
Code on top of it and start, uh, asking

00:49:31.819 --> 00:49:35.792
it what r- needs remediation and how
to do those, and then deploy them

00:49:35.814 --> 00:49:38.856
incrementally, uh, testing each as you go.

00:49:39.214 --> 00:49:42.115
Um, 'cause you don't wanna
do a bunch of changes.

00:49:42.115 --> 00:49:43.478
Just change one variable at a time.

00:49:43.484 --> 00:49:43.498
Right.

00:49:44.303 --> 00:49:46.507
But you can do it, and I've, and
I've used it to great effect.

00:49:46.977 --> 00:49:47.846
Joe: Awesome, awesome.

00:49:48.221 --> 00:49:48.769
Thanks so much.

